{
  "about": "Local paths on the file route (FILE, LAYER): an inherited output that an entry's output patches field by field, a written input path refused in its written form before any source profile is read, and a URI source refused where its layer is read. Moved from file_resources.rs's local_paths module, whose remaining tests prove the resources' captures and resolved locations, which no operation reports. The written-path cases compared each issue's phase, condition, rule, spec paths and context; the inherited-output cases compared the count, the condition and the spec paths of the fields an entry's output left out when it replaced the inherited one whole. An output now composes field by field (LAYER-5, LAYER-6), so an entry that writes only `relative_to` or only `path` keeps the inherited `path`, `columns` and `report`, and the composed output carries every required field (LAYER-7), so the static check reports nothing. A parent spelled with an empty segment, a trailing separator, a network share's root or a backslash is refused as resource_path_not_normalized at parent (FILE-5, LAYER-2), naming the path as written and the layer that writes it, by check and build alike, before any parent is resolved. Moved from the local file half of host_resources.rs::a_malformed_parent_spelling_is_refused_as_written_on_every_route, which compared the one issue's phase, condition, spec paths and context on each route; that test keeps the local resolve and prepare routes and every route through a caller's resolver. A project file the caller passes is the host's own path, not a written project_path (FILE-1, PROJECT-1): under a directory whose name holds a backslash, a file name character on Unix and a separator on Windows, it is read as the host names it, and the check and the build report nothing. So is an entry or a project file whose own name holds a backslash: on Unix it is read beside its sibling src.csv, and on Windows, where the backslash stages a directory, beside the copy there. So is one whose own name holds a colon, a file name character on Unix and not a URI scheme there (FILE-5 judges a written project_path), even a name that begins as a scheme does, such as file:spec.yaml; Windows cannot name such a file, so only Unix replays that case. An output path or report file is a `project_path` like an input path, so check and build refuse one written with a URI scheme as resource_path_uri_scheme, and one with a backslash as resource_path_not_normalized, at its field, under validation, before any data is read (FILE-5, FILE-11).",
  "documents": {
    "base_output": "yamaa: \"1.0\"\ntype: spec\ndomain: BASE\nkeys: [ID]\ninput: {SRC: data.csv}\ncolumns: [{name: ID, type: int, derivation: SRC.ID}]\noutput: {path: result.csv, relative_to: entry, columns: [ID], report: report.csv}\n",
    "written_path": "yamaa: \"1.0\"\ntype: spec\ndomain: TEST\nkeys: [ID]\ninput: {SRC: SOURCE}\ncolumns: [{name: ID, type: int, derivation: SRC.ID}]\noutput: {path: output.csv, columns: [ID]}\n"
  },
